<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
    <title>{{.FrontendName}} | {{.ProjectName}}</title>
    <!--
      Runtime configuration for THIS environment: config.js assigns
      window.__FORGE_CONFIG__, which src/lib/config_gen.ts reads. It has to
      be a plain, blocking, classic <script> in <head> so the global exists
      before the module bundle evaluates. A type="module" script is deferred,
      which is exactly too late.

      This is what lets one built bundle promote between environments: Vite
      inlines import.meta.env.VITE_* at BUILD time, which freezes an artifact
      to the env it was built for. config.js is served beside the bundle
      instead, so a different environment is a different file, not a rebuild.
      In dev, `forge generate` / `forge env up` write it into public/. A
      Firebase or own-bucket deploy writes it per env. A hosted StaticSite
      gets it from the control plane after every sync.

      %BASE_URL% is Vite's `base` (always ending in "/"), so the script
      resolves under a sub-path mount too. Vite leaves a public-dir script
      without type="module" untouched, so this is never bundled.
    -->
    <script src="%BASE_URL%config.js"></script>
    <!--
      Theme, applied BEFORE first paint.

      Without this the page paints the light palette, the bundle loads, an
      effect reads localStorage and only then swaps to dark — a white flash
      on every load for every dark-mode user. It has to be a synchronous
      inline script in <head>: a module script is deferred, which is exactly
      too late.

      It only sets the attribute for an EXPLICIT choice. Left absent, the
      `prefers-color-scheme` block in index.css applies, so a user on
      "system" is already correct with no JS at all.

      The storage key is duplicated from THEME_STORAGE_KEY in
      src/lib/theme/use-theme.ts — this script cannot import, so a contract
      test asserts the two spellings stay identical.
    -->
    <script>
      (function () {
        try {
          var t = localStorage.getItem("forge-theme");
          if (t === "dark" || t === "light") {
            document.documentElement.setAttribute("data-theme", t);
          }
        } catch (e) {}
      })();
    </script>
  </head>
  <body>
    <div id="root"></div>
    <script type="module" src="/src/main.tsx"></script>
  </body>
</html>
